PATCH 334: the flight-model per-object ANIMATION-METHOD family (ab03-dispatched) -- object read from the
WRONG param slot / near-offset base-loss.  Fixes the TARGET-LOCK method FUN_1000_aa08 (the crash the task
named -- the sibling of patch 328's ab88 branch) + its LOS-query builders e1f0/e21c + bearing b112, and the
proximity/collision-slot method FUN_1000_a9b9 (the first crash to MIGRATE once aa08 stopped faulting).

THE CRASH (exact chains, FIST_SEGV_BT+FIST_SEGV_EBP + nm/addr2line, default `make native` AZER1 ACCEPT run
BATTLES(160,100)->OK(205,128)->ACCEPT(40,186), FIST_COOP_TICK=1 FIST_TICK_HZ=25000):
  aa08:  459a -> c0ca -> c0e5 -> 7c1d -> ab03 -> b011 -> aa08,  fault-addr ~0xc, EIP FUN_1000_aa08+0x3d
         (`DAT_2000_5938 = *param_5`), ~1-2/24 ACCEPT runs.
  a9b9:  ...7c1d -> ab03 -> b053 -> a9b9,  fault-addr ~0xfe74, EIP FUN_1000_a9b9+0x17 (surfaced once aa08 fixed).
ROOT CAUSE: ab03 (patch 246) dispatches its frame-phase per-object method via a NEAR indirect call
`call *[DGROUP:0x98dc|0x98fc + idx]` keeping the OBJECT live in DI and threads it to the callee as arg0 (a
DGROUP NEAR OFFSET).  index 6 == 0xb011 = `call 0xf69:0xb378` = FUN_1000_aa08 (b011 forwards its params);
index 13 == 0xb053 = `call 0xf69:0xb329` = FUN_1000_a9b9.  aa08's asm reads its object from DI, but Ghidra
bound aa08's DI varnode to param_5 while b011 forwards arg0 as param_1 -> aa08 dereferenced a GARBAGE
param_5; a9b9 gets the object in param_1 (correct slot) but Ghidra used it as a HOST pointer with NO DGROUP
rebase.  Both are the class documented in docs/stage1.md as "methods that read their object from the
uninitialised param_5 / a base-lost near offset instead of the rebased object" -- the same class patch 328
fixed on the ab88->ab91->0541 branch.

FIXES (each asm-verified vs re_out/fist_dat_image.bin; DS=SS=DGROUP=0x1c00; CS=0):

(a) FUN_1000_aa08 (asm 0x1aa08-0x1ab7e) -- the per-object TARGET-LOCK / nearest-hostile selection method.
    Reconstructed 1:1: object = g_mem+0x1c000+di (di = the near offset delivered in param_1); every
    [di+off]/[si+off] field, the four word0*2-indexed per-type tables (-0x6764/-0x68d2/-0x68ca/-0x68c2) and
    the DGROUP:0xdfbc candidate-list walk rebased to the DGROUP base; the DAT_2000_59xx scratch globals
    kept (already correctly based).  Calls the LOS query e1f0 + the range helper 08e8 (== a17e's sole
    trampoline target, `a17e: call 0x8e8; retf`) + the bearing b112 with the object/candidate HOST pointers,
    and bf3c with the NEAR offset (it compares to the DGROUP-offset player DAT_2000_2d34).  The range word is
    08e8's stored DAT_1000_c458/c45a (== its dx:ax return, asm 0x91d/0x920).

(b) FUN_0000_e1f0 (asm 0xe1f0-0xe1ff) -- passes the CANDIDATE (param_6) + OBJECT (param_7) HOST pointers to
    e21c and returns its result.  Ghidra CONCAT22-mangled the candidate, typed param_6/param_7 uint16_t
    (truncating the host pointers) and dropped the return.  (Its other caller a77e is the pinned base-lost
    sibling target-lock -- unchanged, and already reached the base-lost e21c before this patch.)

(c) FUN_0000_e21c (asm 0xe21c-0xe2a4) -- the LOS/range-query builder: writes the candidate/object coords
    into the extender TCB (param_2 = DAT_2000_aa2c) and POSTS op 0x58 via e339.  int*N pointer-scaling
    (piVar2+2 => +8 where the asm reads +4) restored to the asm's +4/+8/+0xc byte offsets; the three
    per-type height-table reads ([di-0x6151]/[di-0x1a78]/[bx-0x1a40] are DGROUP near offsets) rebased;
    return type void -> int (Ghidra dropped e339's result).

(d) FUN_0000_b112 (asm 0xb112-0xb122) -- bearing(best->object) via 0731 (patch 328, host-ptr) + the dropped
    `add ah,0x80` bias + the dropped return.  Reached only once a target is locked.

(e) FUN_1000_a9b9 (asm 0x1a9b9-0x1aa07) -- the per-object PROXIMITY/COLLISION-slot registration method.
    Object = g_mem+0x1c000+di (param_1 near offset); the DGROUP:0x6d3a/0x6d3c collision-slot table
    read+store (Ghidra read the WORD entry as int** -> host pointer) rebased + WORD store widths; the
    candidate `[si+off]` reads rebased.

